        We are left to prove that $Q'\le P(21E)$. Consider the projection of $x$, $y$, and the $Q'$ domains above in the top-level coordinate space $\mathcal C S$: see \Cref{fig:passingup} to understand the situation. All $g_i\ell$ are nested in $V$, so $\mathrm{d}_S(\rho^{g_i\ell}_S, \rho^{V}_S)\le E$ by consistency. Furthermore, $\mathrm{d}_S(\rho^{g_i\ell}_S, \rho^{g_i\ell^\bot}_S)\le 2E$ by \Cref{lem:DHS}. Now let $\gamma =[\pi_S(x), \pi_S(y)]\subseteq \mathcal C S$ be a geodesic; since $\mathrm{d}_{\mathcal C(g_i\ell)^\bot}(x,y)> 3E$, $\gamma$ intersects the $E$-neighbourhood of $\rho^{g_i\ell}_S$ by bounded geodesic image, and therefore the $(6E)$-neighbourhood of $\rho^V_S$ (here we are using that relative projections have diameter at most $E$). Let $p\in \mathcal C S$ be the first point on $\gamma$ within distance $9E$ from $\rho^V_S$. If $p=x$ define $x'=x$; else, let $x'\in G$ project $E$-close to $p$ in $\mathcal C S$, which exists because $\pi_S$ is $E$-coarsely-onto. Notice that any geodesic in $\mathcal C S$ connecting $x$ and $x'$ avoids the $(6E)$-neighbourhood of $\rho^V_S$ (here we are using that triangles are $E$-slim in $\mathcal C S$), and therefore $\mathrm{d}_{\mathcal C(g_i\ell)^\bot}(x,x')\le E$ for all the $Q'$ domains by the bounded geodesic image theorem. Now define $y'$ analogously on the side of $y$, so that $\mathrm{d}_{\mathcal C(g_i\ell)^\bot}(y,y')\le E$ for all the $Q'$ domains. Notice that every $W\in \mathfrak S-\{S\}$ containing some $g_i\ell^\bot$ is bounded by \Cref{notation:tweakable_hhg}.\eqref{need:orth_compl}, and therefore has diameter at most $E$ by the bounded domain dichotomy, \Cref{rem:BDD}. Notice also that 
        \[\mathrm{d}_S(x',y')\le \mathrm{d}_S(x',\rho^V_S)+\mathrm{d}_S(y',\rho^V_S)+E\le 21E,\]
        so the two facts combine to give that $\mathrm{d}_W(x',y')\le 21E$ whenever $W$ properly contains some $g_i\ell^\bot$. However, for all $i=1,\ldots, Q'$, we have
        \[\mathrm{d}_{\mathcal C(g_i\ell)^\bot}(x',y')\ge \mathrm{d}_{\mathcal C(g_i\ell)^\bot}(x,y) -\mathrm{d}_{\mathcal C(g_i\ell)^\bot}(x,x')-\mathrm{d}_{\mathcal C(g_i\ell)^\bot}(y,y')>E.\]
        The original passing up axiom, applied to the collection $\{g_i \ell^\bot\}_{i=1,\ldots, Q'}$ inside $S$, then implies that $Q'\le P(21E)$, as required.

         \medskip\textbf{(Equivariance)} The $G$-action on $\mathfrak S$ is the same as before, and in particular it is cofinite. Moreover, as pointed out above, all projections to and between domains are $G$-equivariant, so the above data defines a HHG structure for $G$.

There is nothing to prove if either $\pi_{\ell}(A)$ or $\pi_{\ell^\bot}(A)$ are unbounded, so suppose otherwise. Taking the gate to $P_{\ell}$ does not change the coordinates in $\mathcal C \ell$ and $\mathcal C \ell^\bot$, so $B\coloneq \mathfrak{g}_\ell(A)$ has bounded projections to $\mathcal C \ell$ and $\mathcal C \ell^\bot$. Now, by construction $\pi_{\ell_\lambda}(A)$ is the projection of $B$ to $L_\lambda$; since $\theta_\lambda=\phi+\lambda\psi\colon L_\lambda\to \mathbb{R}$ is a quasi-isometry, it is enough to show that both $\phi$ and $\psi$ are bounded on $B$, when we identify the latter with a subset of $\operatorname{Stab}_{G}(\ell)$.  Now we analyse one quasicocycle at a time:
            \begin{itemize}
                \item Recall that $\phi$ is the Busemann quasicocycle associated to the action on $\mathcal C\ell$, as in \Cref{ex:busemann}, which depends on the choice of $i\in \operatorname{Stab}_{G}(\ell)$ and has the property that $\phi(ki)=\phi(k)$ for all $k\in K$. Now let $x\in  B$, and let $x'$ be either $x$ if $x\in K$, or $xi^{-1}$ if $x\not\in K$, so that $\phi(x')=\phi(x)$ while  $\pi_{\ell}(x')\sim \pi_\ell(x)$ belongs to a bounded subset. If we fix $x_0\in B$, for every other $x$ the difference $x'(x_0')^{-1}$ preserves a bounded subset, and therefore acts with uniformly bounded orbits on $\mathcal C\ell$ as it preserves orientation. Hence $\phi (x'(x_0')^{-1})=0$ for all $x\in  B$, meaning that $\phi(B)\sim \phi(x_0')$ is bounded.
                \item Furthermore, $\mathcal C\ell^\bot =\operatorname{Cay}\left(\operatorname{Stab}_{G}(\ell),X\right)$ and $\psi$ is $X$-coarsely-Lipschitz, hence $\psi(B)$ is bounded as so is $\pi_{\ell^\bot}(B)$.\qedhere
            \end{itemize}

The proof will follow the same lines as that of \cite[Theorem 7.1]{short_HHG:I}. For every $\lambda\in \mathbb{R}$ let $\mu_\lambda$ be the coarse median structure associated to the HHG structure $(G,\mathfrak S_\lambda)$, which is constructed as in \Cref{ex:coarse_median_from_HHS}. Our goal is to show that, as we let $\lambda$ vary in $\mathbb{R}$, the coarse medians $\mu_\lambda$ are all non-equivalent. Fix $\lambda_1\neq \lambda_2$, and recall from \Cref{construction:newline} that for $i=1,2$ the quasiline $L_{\lambda_i}$ is constructed via \Cref{lem:quasiline_from_qc} from the quasicocycle
    \[\theta_{\lambda_i}\coloneq\phi+\lambda_i\psi\colon \operatorname{Stab}_{G}(\ell)\to \mathbb{R}.\]
    Fix $g\in \operatorname{Stab}_{G}(\ell)$ with $\psi(g)\neq 0$, which exists because $\psi$ is a non-trivial homogeneous quasimorphism, and consider triples of the form $1,T^k, g^l$, for some integers $k$ and $l$. For $i=1,2$ let \[m_i=\mu_{\lambda_i}(1,T^k, g^l)\in Z.\] We want to show that, by choosing appropriate values of $k$ and $l$, the quantity \[\min\{\mathrm{d}_{L_{\lambda_1}}(m_1, m_2),\mathrm{d}_{L_{\lambda_2}}(m_1, m_2)\}\] can be made arbitrarily large. This will imply that $m_1$ and $m_2$ can be made arbitrarily far in $Z$, because the projections from $Z$ to both ${L_{\lambda_1}}$ and ${L_{\lambda_2}}$ are coarsely Lipschitz. 


To determine the projection of $m_i$ to the quasiline $L_{\lambda_i}$, we have to determine which of the numbers $\theta_{\lambda_i}(1),\theta_{\lambda_i}(T^k), \theta_{\lambda_i}(g^l)$ lies between the other two, because then the projection of $m_i$ will coarsely coincide with that of the corresponding $x_i\in\{1,T^k, g^l\}$. Since $\theta_{\lambda_1}(g)\neq \theta_{\lambda_2}(g)$, we can choose values of $k$ and $l$ such that $x_1\neq x_2$; thus, without loss of generality, $x_1$ is either $1$ or $T^k$, and in particular $\theta_{\lambda_1}(x_1)=\theta_{\lambda_2}(x_1)=\phi(x_1)$. 

\begin{claim}
    $m_1$ is uniformly close to $x_1$, i.e. their distance is bounded independently of $k$ and $l$.
\end{claim}
\begin{claimproof}
    By the uniqueness axiom for the structure $(G,\mathfrak S_{\lambda_2})$, it is enough to show that $\pi_U(m_1)$ uniformly coarsely coincides with $\pi_U(x_1)$ for every domain $U$ (where projections are with respect to the structure $(G,\mathfrak S_{\lambda_2})$). We already know that $\pi_{L_{\lambda_1}}(m_1)$ uniformly coarsely agrees with $\pi_{L_{\lambda_1}}(x_1)$, so by how the coarse median was constructed in \Cref{ex:coarse_median_from_HHS} it is enough to show that $1$ and $T^k$ project uniformly close to every other domain $U$ (in the structure $(G,\mathfrak S_{\lambda_1})$). If $U\bot \ell$ then this is because $\langle z\rangle$ acts with uniformly bounded orbits on the coordinate space $\mathcal C U$, by the ``furthermore" part of \Cref{prop:new_hhg_structures}; if instead $\ell\pitchfork  U$ or $\ell\sqsubseteq U$ then this follows from e.g. \cite[Proposition 2.17]{HRSS_3manifold}.
\end{claimproof}
We can now prove that $\mathrm{d}_{L_{\lambda_2}}(m_1, m_2)$ can be made arbitrarily large. Indeed, by the above Claim and by the definition of $x_2$, we can replace $m_1$ by $x_1$ and $m_2$ by $x_2$; then, since $\theta_{\lambda_2}\colon L_{\lambda_2}\to \mathbb{R}$ is a quasi-isometry, it is enough to notice that the difference 
$$|\theta_{\lambda_2}(x_1)-\theta_{\lambda_2}(x_2)|=|\theta_{\lambda_1}(x_1)-\theta_{\lambda_2}(x_2)|$$
can be made arbitrarily large.

The HHG structure for $A_\Gamma$ comes from the action on the universal cover of the Salvetti complex $S_\Gamma$. Let $\mathcal F$ be the collection of full subgraphs of $\Gamma$ containing $\Gamma$ and all non-empty subgraphs of links of vertices, which is clearly closed under non-empty intersections and therefore yields a HHG structure by \cite[Proposition 8.3]{HHS_I}. The index set $\mathfrak S$ is again the collection $\{gA_\Lambda\}/\sim$, where $g\in A_\Gamma$, $\Lambda\in \mathcal F$, and both $\mathcal F$ and the equivalence relation $\sim$ are defined as in the Coxeter case. The factored contact graph of $[A_\Lambda]$ satisfies the following properties:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If $\Lambda$ has at least two vertices and is not a proper join then $\mathcal C [A_\Lambda]$ is unbounded.
        \item If $\Lambda$ is a single vertex, then $\mathcal C [A_\Lambda]$ is a line, on which $A_\Lambda\cong \mathbb{Z}$ acts geometrically;
    \end{itemize}
    The proof of \Cref{thmintro_coxeter} now runs verbatim, with the following minor adjustments. The domain $[A_{\{v\}}]$ is $\sqsubseteq$-minimal because $\{v\}$ is minimal under inclusion among subgraphs of $\Gamma$. Moreover, we set $T=v$, and notice that $A_{\operatorname{Link}_{\Gamma}\left(v\right)}=\operatorname{Stab}_{A_\Gamma}(v)$ acts non-elementarily on the unbounded hyperbolic space $\mathcal C [A_{\operatorname{Link}_{\Gamma}\left(v\right)}]$ since ${\operatorname{Link}_{\Gamma}\left(v\right)}$ is not a single vertex nor a proper join.
